# skale-cron crontab — supercronic format (standard 5-field cron + command).
#
# One versioned file holding the scheduled jobs for every project in the
# Skale-Club org. Each job used to be its own GitHub Actions workflow
# (`.github/workflows/*.yml` in the source repo) billed a full minute per
# run even though the actual `curl` took seconds; moving the trigger here
# runs it on the Hetzner VPS that was already doing the work, for free.
#
# All times are UTC (see .env.example TZ) so these cadences mean exactly
# what the GitHub `cron:` expressions they replace meant.
#
# Command form: tick.sh <project> <job_name> <url> [expected_interval_seconds] [max_seconds]
# <project> (XPHERE, XKEDULE, SKALECLUB, XTIMATOR, ...) drives which
# <PROJECT>_CRON_SECRET and which per-project Telegram bot tick.sh resolves
# automatically — see tick.sh's header for the full resolution order and
# the CRON_SECRET_OVERRIDE / HTTP_METHOD escape hatches used below.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# xphere (source: xphere/.github/workflows/*.yml)
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# campaign-tick — advances stalled Vapi voice campaigns whose contact list
# spans more than one batch (the end-of-call webhook never re-fires the next
# batch on its own). Kept at */5, matching the GitHub cadence: the endpoint
# is idempotent/atomic per contact, but firing tighter than every 5 minutes
# skews the effective calls_per_minute pacing (see the original workflow's
# comment on batch math). max_seconds 120 matches the workflow's --max-time.
*/5 * * * * /app/tick.sh XPHERE campaign-tick "${XPHERE_BASE_URL}/api/cron/campaign-tick" 300 120

# calendar-tick — fires meeting.starts_in / meeting.ended workflow triggers.
# The endpoint is idempotent (UNIQUE constraint on scheduled_workflow_ticks),
# so */5 is safe and matches the smallest reminder offset (-5m) the route's
# window logic covers.
*/5 * * * * /app/tick.sh XPHERE calendar-tick "${XPHERE_BASE_URL}/api/cron/calendar-tick" 300 60

# twilio-sms-reconcile — backfills inbound SMS that missed the webhook.
# Plan cadence tightened from */5 to */10: safe because the query's
# lookbackMinutes=180 (3h) comfortably covers a 10-minute gap between runs.
# Query params kept exactly as the workflow used them.
*/10 * * * * /app/tick.sh XPHERE twilio-sms-reconcile "${XPHERE_BASE_URL}/api/cron/twilio-sms-reconcile?lookbackMinutes=180&maxPagesPerNumber=5" 600 120

# website-analyzer — processes up to 10 pending/stale prospect website
# analyses per run. Cadence unchanged at */10 (already the target) so
# newly ingested prospects get analyzed promptly.
*/10 * * * * /app/tick.sh XPHERE website-analyzer "${XPHERE_BASE_URL}/api/cron/website-analyzer" 600 60

# global-knowledge-notion — drains the durable Notion sync queue. This job
# legitimately runs 88-103s (it's why the GitHub workflow bypassed
# Cloudflare's 100s proxy timeout via --resolve to the origin IP). On this
# VPS the request can go straight to the container's internal address once
# XPHERE_BASE_URL is switched to it (see README "internal networking"),
# which sidesteps the Cloudflare 524 risk entirely. max_seconds 180 gives
# headroom above the observed 103s ceiling.
*/10 * * * * ORIGIN_HOSTNAME=origin.xphere.app /app/tick.sh XPHERE global-knowledge-notion "${XPHERE_BASE_URL}/api/cron/global-knowledge-notion" 600 180

# scrape-reviews — daily Google Reviews scrape (SerpAPI) across every active
# google_business_profile. xphere-specific exception: authenticates with
# OPERATOR_AUTOMATION_SECRET instead of XPHERE_CRON_SECRET (matches the
# separate secret the source workflow used), and is a POST, not a GET —
# both overridden inline for this one line only.
0 6 * * * CRON_SECRET_OVERRIDE="$OPERATOR_AUTOMATION_SECRET" HTTP_METHOD=POST /app/tick.sh XPHERE scrape-reviews "${XPHERE_BASE_URL}/api/reviews/scrape" 86400 120

# --- NOT migrated: meta-capi-worker, meta-audience-sync -------------------
# Both `xphere/.github/workflows/meta-capi-worker.yml` and
# `meta-audience-sync.yml` check out the repo and run a TypeScript script
# (`npx tsx scripts/meta-*.ts`) directly against Supabase + the Meta Graph
